WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.657
Your document has important things to say.

2
00:00:02.657 --> 00:00:05.015
Unfortunately, it is a document.

3
00:00:05.015 --> 00:00:06.299
It has no mouth.

4
00:00:06.299 --> 00:00:12.986
Meet Explain-to-Me: a local-first toolkit that turns your words into narrated, captioned explainer videos.

5
00:00:12.986 --> 00:00:14.483
Tiny movie studio.

6
00:00:14.483 --> 00:00:16.341
Extremely sensible shoes.

7
00:00:16.341 --> 00:00:18.103
Here is the conveyor belt.

8
00:00:18.103 --> 00:00:22.240
Start with Markdown, plain text, or a reviewed production manifest.

9
00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:27.854
The toolkit prepares narration, measures the actual speech, then builds the timeline around it.

10
00:00:27.854 --> 00:00:30.375
Diagrams, code cards, captions.

11
00:00:30.375 --> 00:00:32.139
Everybody arrives together.

12
00:00:32.139 --> 00:00:34.125
Nobody gets shoved off the bus.

13
00:00:34.125 --> 00:00:40.009
For version zero point two, bring a Mac, Node twenty-four or newer, Git, and F F mpeg.

14
00:00:40.009 --> 00:00:41.612
Clone the repository.

15
00:00:41.612 --> 00:00:43.534
Install the pinned dependencies.

16
00:00:43.534 --> 00:00:46.713
Download the rendering browser once, then run doctor.

17
00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:48.944
The README has the exact commands.

18
00:00:48.944 --> 00:00:50.513
Doctor checks the tools.

19
00:00:50.513 --> 00:00:52.425
It does not check your dance moves.

20
00:00:52.425 --> 00:00:56.328
Make your first video with the bundled manifest and Apple speech.

21
00:00:56.328 --> 00:00:58.656
No extra language model required.

22
00:00:58.656 --> 00:01:03.594
Choose a fresh output folder, run the build, and open video dot M P four.

23
00:01:03.594 --> 00:01:04.996
Congratulations!

24
00:01:04.996 --> 00:01:07.899
Your terminal has entered its film director era.

25
00:01:07.899 --> 00:01:10.663
Next, hand it your own Markdown or text file.

26
00:01:10.663 --> 00:01:13.609
Template mode selects source material directly.

27
00:01:13.609 --> 00:01:16.498
Want local model direction and a smoother voice?

28
00:01:16.498 --> 00:01:21.212
Install the optional director and Kokoro models, then choose those backends.

29
00:01:21.212 --> 00:01:24.520
Setup downloads the tools; inference runs locally.

30
00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:26.749
Always review what the model writes.

31
00:01:26.749 --> 00:01:32.662
Your output folder includes the movie, narration audio, captions, manifest, and a report.

32
00:01:32.662 --> 00:01:34.884
Keep the manifest to revise the script.

33
00:01:34.884 --> 00:01:36.149
Check the report.

34
00:01:36.149 --> 00:01:38.062
Watch and listen before sharing.

35
00:01:38.062 --> 00:01:45.133
Automated checks can catch broken timing; they cannot tell whether your explanation is secretly about a potato.

36
00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:46.992
Prefer a robot assistant?

37
00:01:46.992 --> 00:01:50.961
Copy the quick installer prompt from the README into your coding agent.

38
00:01:50.961 --> 00:01:57.212
It sets up the pinned release, checks prerequisites, and keeps your private stories outside the software checkout.

39
00:01:57.212 --> 00:01:58.870
You review the finished movie.

40
00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:02.197
The robot gets a tiny imaginary director chair.

41
00:02:02.197 --> 00:02:06.025
Version zero point two ships the technical whiteboard style.

42
00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:09.556
These cartoons and this music are a custom release showcase.

43
00:02:09.556 --> 00:02:10.704
Grab the code.

44
00:02:10.704 --> 00:02:12.360
Explain something useful.

45
00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.697
Make somebody go, oh, now I get it!

46
00:02:14.697 --> 00:02:15.868
Explain-to-Me.

47
00:02:15.868 --> 00:02:17.269
Less wall of text.

48
00:02:17.269 --> 00:02:18.272
More aha.
